124: \begin{abstract}
125: A minimal presentation of the cohomology ring of the flag manifold $GL_n/B$ was given in [A.~Borel, 1953]. This presentation was extended
126: by [E.~Akyildiz--A.~Lascoux--P.~Pragacz, 1992] to a non-minimal one for all Schubert varieties. Work of [V.~Gasharov--V.~Reiner, 2002] gave a short, \emph{i.e.} polynomial-size,
127: presentation for a subclass of Schubert varieties that includes the smooth ones. In [V.~Reiner--A.~Woo--A.~Yong, 2011], a general shortening was found; it implies an exponential upper bound of $2^n$ on the number of generators required. That work states
128: a minimality
129: conjecture whose significance would be an exponential lower bound of $\frac{\sqrt{2}^{n+2}}{\sqrt{\pi n}}$ on the number
130: of generators needed in worst case, giving the first obstructions to short presentations. We prove the minimality conjecture. Our proof uses the Hopf algebra structure of the ring of symmetric functions.
131: \end{abstract}

300: The following definition records the assumptions on the parameters used in the preceding considerations.
301: \begin{definition}
302: Call an integer tuple $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ \emph{valid} if $n,r,i,j,a,b,N\geq 1$ with
303: \[r<n,\quad a+j\leq r,\quad a+i\leq n-r,\quad b\leq i,j,\quad\mbox{and}\quad N\leq ab. \]
304: \end{definition}

387: \begin{definition}
388: \label{def:cp}
389: We define a linear endomorphism $\mathrm{CP}:\overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}\to \overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}$ as follows.
390: For a simple tensor $\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_\mu\in \overline{\Lambda}\otimes\overline{\Lambda}$, define
391: \begin{align}
392: \mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_\lambda\otimes \overline{s}_\mu) = \begin{cases}
393: \overline{\Delta(s_{\lambda})} - (\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes 1) &\mbox{ if $\overline{s}_\mu= 1$},\\
394: \overline{\Delta(s_{\lambda})}(1\otimes \overline{s}_{\mu}) &\mbox{ if $\overline{s}_\mu\neq 1$}.
395: \end{cases}
396: \end{align}
397: Define $\mathrm{CP}$ on all of $\overline{\Lambda}\otimes \overline{\Lambda}$ by extending linearly.

625: \begin{proof}It follows from Proposition~\ref{clm:2} that during the algorithm, the only cancellation that occurs in an update
626: \begin{equation}
627: \label{eqn:theupdate}
628: \xi^{(m)}=\xi^{(m-1)}-\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))
629: \end{equation}
630: is the cancellation of the term $\gamma\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})$ in $\xi^{(m-1)}$, and its negative in $\gamma\mathrm{CP}(\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}}))$. Moreover, the algorithm only can
631: eliminate a term $\gamma\overline{s}_{\lambda}\otimes (\overline{s}_{\mu^{(1)}}\cdots \overline{s}_{\mu^{(k)}})$ with $|\lambda|>1$ by the update step
632: (\ref{eqn:theupdate}). Therefore the order in which we compute the update steps (\ref{eqn:theupdate}) does not effect the output.\end{proof}

790: \begin{theorem}
791: \label{thm:inconsistent}
792: For any valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$, the equations
793: \begin{equation}
794: \label{eqn:all-equations}
795: \left\{ \linear_{\varphi}(\nu) = d_{\nu}\mid \nu\in \decomp_{\varphi} \right\}
796: \end{equation}
797: are inconsistent whenever $d_{\nu}=0$ for all wide $\nu$, and $d_{\rho}\neq 0$ for some tall $\rho$.
798: \end{theorem}

326: \begin{lemma}
327: \label{lem:implication-of-syzygy}
328: Suppose for some valid $\varphi=(n,r,i,j,a,b,N)$ there is a homogeneous syzygy of degree $N$ in $\overline{\Lambda}$ of the form
329: \[\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|=N}}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}f_{\lambda}=\sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}f_{\lambda}\overline{s}_{(i^j,\lambda)}\]
330: with the left-hand side nonzero.
331: Then the decomposable equations
332: \begin{equation}
333: \label{eqn:decomposable}
334: \biggr\{ \sum_{\substack{\lambda\subseteq b^a\\|\lambda|<N }}\sum_{\substack{\theta\subseteq (n-r)^r\\|\theta|=N-|\lambda|}} c_{(i^j,\lambda),\theta}^{\nu} \, A_{\lambda,\theta}=\chi(\nu)\,\biggr| \,\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi} \biggr\}
335: \end{equation}
336: are consistent, where $\chi(\nu)\in\mathbb{Z}$ is defined for $\nu\in \decomp_{\varphi}$ by
337: \[\chi(\nu)=\begin{cases}
338: f_{\nu_B} &\mbox{ if $\nu$ is tall,}\\
339: 0 &\mbox{ if $\nu$ is wide.}
340: \end{cases} \]
341: \end{lemma}
342: \begin{proof}
343: The above considerations show that the lemma is true if we replace (\ref{eqn:decomposable}) with the superset (\ref{eqn:eqs}). Clearly, removing equations from a consistent linear system yields a consistent linear system.
344: \end{proof}
